  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1144 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nA persistent upper high was located over the southwest United \nStates earlier this evening with several weak upper level \ndisturbances rotating around this upper high. Across eastern \nColorado and western Kansas a trough of low pressure at the \nsurface extended from southeast Colorado into north central \nKansas. 850Mb temperatures at 00z Sunday from eastern Colorado \ninto western Kansas ranged from 32 to 34C and 700mb temperatures\nwere between 16 and 18c. These unseasonably warm temperatures \ncombined with deep mixing on Sunday resulted in highs ranging \nfrom 105 to around 110. Also given afternoon dewpoints in the \nlower 50s the afternoon heat index values were around 105 to \nnear 110, mainly east of highway 283. These hot temperatures \ntoday were 3-5F warmer than the NBM guidance with the 75th to \n90th percentile for highs on Sunday verifying better. Given \nthis and that the 24 hour trend in 850mb and 700mb temperatures \nwill change/decrease by less than 2C it appears that afternoon \ntemperatures across southwest Kansas will be near or only a few \ndegrees cooler compared to what we had on Sunday. This would \nsupport more of the NBM 75th to 90th percentile for highs on \nMonday than the cooler current NBM guidance. Also do not expect \ndewpoints to be much lower than what we had today also given the\nongoing southerly flow. Based on all this and that these \nconditions warranted a heat advisory yesterday will go ahead and\nissue another heat advisory for all but north central Kansas. \nReason for holding off across portions of north central Kansas \nis due to the uncertainty on how quickly temperatures may \nrecover given the potential for some cool outflow air spreading \ninto this region from a cluster of thunderstorms that will be \nmoving across north central Kansas overnight. Will monitor this \nover the next few hours and if it looks like this outflow will \nnot have much impact with temperatures on Monday will expand the\nheat advisory northward.\n\nAlso on Monday the southwest United States upper high will\nexpand eastward which will improve large scale subsidence across\nwestern Kansas. As a result despite the possibility over \nanother upper wave exiting the Central Rockies and moving out \ninto the plains late Monday the probability of convection \ncurrently appears lower than what west central and north central\nKansas experienced over the weekend period. The chance for \nstorms Monday evening is not zero but below 15%. Should a storm\nor two develop it will be along the surface trough that will \nstill be located across eastern Colorado and northeast Kansas. \nIsolated wind gusts of 50-60mph will still be the primary hazard\ngiven the high cloud bases and large low level lapse rates. \n\nLittle relief in the heat can be expect Tuesday and Wednesday \nas the unseasonably warm 850mb temperatures remain in place. \nHighs once again in the triple digits will be possible. Both \ndays the NBM has a 30-60% chance for highs to exceed 105 across \nextreme southwest Kansas, as well as area east of Liberal along\nthe Oklahoma border and across much of south central Kansas. \n\nChanges in this weather pattern will begin to take shape during\nthe last half of the work week as a cold front drops south into\nwestern Kansas and the upper ridge over the plains shifts \neastwards as an upper level trough develop/move into over the \nwestern United States. This will allow for a more favorable \nenvironment for some monsoonal moisture to return to the Central\nPlains. Upper level waves embedded in the developing west southwest\nflow along with improving moisture and a surface boundary \nin/near southwest Kansas will result in improving chances for \nthunderstorms late week and into the weekend. Improving\nprecipitation chances along increase PWATs by the weekend also \nwould point towards more favorable conditions for a period of \nwidespread beneficial rainfall. \n\nThis pattern change will also bring an end to the extended \nperiod of heat that southwest Kansas will experience during the \nfirst half of the work week.",
